From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload PPPoE downloads

A download through a PPPoE interface arrives on the WAN port in a PPPoE
session, which the flow entry cannot express: it has no PPPoE field. The
PPE terminates a session in its session table, which gives the frames of
one session from one access concentrator on one port an L3 interface of
their own; a flow keyed on that L3 interface then matches only them.

PPPoE flows need a second module parameter, pppoe_offload (default off),
on top of flow_offload: the session match has no VLAN field, so the
session ID from its concentrator's MAC on another VLAN of the port, or
untagged, also reaches its flows, which software would not forward;
setting it accepts that. Clearing it takes every session out of hardware.

With the flowtable's FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE match (generic pending
patch 714), accept a direction of a flow received through a PPPoE
interface (the reply of a connection routed out through it, or a port
forward into the LAN) when its destination before translation is that
interface's own address and the interface's forward path is exactly the
rule's: the session, on a VLAN of the EDMA port or on the port itself.
Program the session (at most four, refcounted by their flows): an
ingress L3 interface (PPPoE termination, IPv4 routing, the port's
MY_MAC, the PPP MTU) and an egress one, then the session entry, its
valid word last, matching the session ID, the port and the
concentrator's MAC; read everything back. Arm the PPPoE header
exceptions (wrong version or type, wrong code, a PPP protocol other than
IP) to redirect and de-accelerate on a flow hit, as the vendor does.

A session goes out of hardware, valid word first, and its flows with it,
when its PPP, VLAN or port device goes down, loses carrier, changes
address, MTU or upper, or goes away, when its last flow goes, and at
disable; a session entry that cannot be cleared keeps its slot out of
use and declines every new flow until a reboot. A generation count keeps
a flow resolved against the old devices from programming a stale
session.

The session entry terminates every frame of the session, not only those
that hit a flow, and no source says how those that miss reach the CPU.
The bench checks that before a session is written on a live line; in
service, an IP frame from the concentrator to this host on the session's
lower device itself (a session frame handed over decapsulated; frames
the core hands over for an upper of that device do not count) takes the
session out of hardware and keeps PPPoE in software until offload is
enabled again, as do two sessions in a row that die within a minute of
going into hardware (a PPP device going down or away; other device
changes and clearing pppoe_offload do not count, nor does a session out
of hardware when its device dies). A session is not used without that
watch, nor while its port or lower device has no carrier. Two sessions
in hardware differing only by VLAN are refused.

Enabling now also checks that L3_ROUTE_CTRL redirects PPPoE multicast to
the CPU, programs the PPPoE header exceptions, and saves the session
entries and L3 interfaces 16-19 for disable to write back, whatever
pppoe_offload says; a session entry found valid then refuses enabling,
as someone else's.

For the bench, /sys/kernel/debug/ppe/offload_pppoe_pin ("<ppp device>
<seconds>") programs a PPP device's session without any flow, for at
most ten minutes: what the session entry alone does to the session's
frames. The pin is held to the same device generation check as an offer,
and goes when its session is taken out of hardware.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
